## Monitoring Patterns

### 1. Connection Health Monitoring
```javascript
// ✅ APPROPRIATE monitoring
class WebSocketService {
  constructor() {
    this.heartbeatInterval = null;
    this.lastHeartbeat = null;
    this.heartbeatTimeout = 30000;
  }
  
  startHeartbeat() {
    this.heartbeatInterval = setInterval(() => {
      if (this.socket && this.socket.connected) {
        this.socket.emit('ping');
        this.lastHeartbeat = Date.now();
      }
    }, 10000);
  }
  
  checkConnectionHealth() {
    if (this.lastHeartbeat && Date.now() - this.lastHeartbeat > this.heartbeatTimeout) {
      console.warn('WebSocket connection may be stale');
      this.reconnect();
    }
  }
}

// ❌ OVER-ENGINEERED monitoring
class WebSocketService {
  constructor() {
    this.metricsCollector = new MetricsCollector({
      connectionMetrics: new ConnectionMetrics(),
      messageMetrics: new MessageMetrics(),
      performanceMetrics: new PerformanceMetrics()
    });
    
    this.healthChecker = new HealthChecker({
      interval: 5000,
      timeout: 10000,
      retries: 3,
      backoff: new ExponentialBackoffStrategy()
    });
  }
}
```

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

### ❌ Over-Engineering Real-time Features
- Complex real-time architecture for simple updates
- Advanced conflict resolution for simple data
- Complex message queuing for basic broadcasting
- Over-optimization of simple WebSocket usage

### ❌ Under-Engineering Real-time Features
- No offline resilience
- No connection monitoring
- No fallback mechanisms
- No performance optimization
- No security considerations

## Scale-Appropriate Real-time Patterns

### ✅ Right-Sized Solutions
- Simple WebSocket broadcasting
- Basic offline resilience
- Simple connection monitoring
- Appropriate rate limiting
- Basic performance optimization
- Focused testing strategy

## Remember: Choose real-time patterns that match the actual requirements and scale, not theoretical maximums or over-engineering patterns.